ctive eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/5611ead246c3e5a26d991603a68f3326b1c381d44eddd116316ca7ea6427fdd8.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/example-5504.json" "file_sha256" "63b346accac7a544e0ebe7a1e4f8355692af7682fcc25ab1877598820b990221"} {"question" "e-5504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}-1},$ $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}1},$ $\\lambda_3=0,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/65491fb3de59e45bffb4774aca31cc25f20f555f18ffafc8d56221c689520380.png)\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}-1},$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}1},$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_3=0.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/3798d5e0b3305070161e2df70796a20a8fcd1e11f11cecdc94d59db520582ca3.png)\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/1448624dbf0dbb227d5e2a3f082dbda389c521d905b1eee1aafe9a2226e25cfd.png)\n\nIn conclusion, we get $a=0,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/example-5504.json" "file_sha256" "63b346accac7a544e0ebe7a1e4f8355692af7682fcc25ab1877598820b990221"} {"question" "e-5505" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/62f202b2fdbe406e4785cc4acf7f2b9aaa09980bc894340716fbbe5600fb8ae9.png)\nDetermine whether $A=PDP^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/example-5505.json" "file_sha256" "37210289359d783ce8bf097a657331c143c4b28aa9814d201e36b2a38ec1c32b"} {"question" "e-5505" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $\\mathbb R.$ \n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )([{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda +2]-12) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -10) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )(\\lambda -5)(\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1=4,$ $\\lambda_2=5,$ and $\\lambda_3=-2.$ Since all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable. \n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/2f943468de2124874c2d806db25c6ca91a2df9a74902af95220e4974e5ef483b.png)\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible  diagonal matrix is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/6f73d40195a91530d761d966db886e9ad2d87d7fc1059a4112a0a2701ce6a72f.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/example-5505.json" "file_sha256" "37210289359d783ce8bf097a657331c143c4b28aa9814d201e36b2a38ec1c32b"} {"question" "e-5506" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following matrices:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/7d992806d0d5b90b65030f8c0080f105f67fbb7b4d3d807c3a719307e0c31e40.png)\nGiven that $D=P^{-1}AP,$ find the first column of the matrix $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/example-5506.json" "file_sha256" "b71888518c3c14b743bcc9a696b363817e28f54df69982927c5323a5e00b9487"} {"question" "e-5506" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are  \n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=-2, \\quad$ $\\lambda_2=-1, \\quad$ $\\lambda_3=2.$ \n\nSo, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=-2.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda_1=-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -9 & -7 & 9 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 14\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -9 & -5 & 9 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -9 & -5 & 9 & 0 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 16 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{8}{3}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{3} & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain $x_2=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get $x_1=x_3.$\nHence, the general solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/fdf2311a1010ed8e8f25623328171687a63ea5da80f6e18a1a8dfbff9e5fae42.png)\n\nTherefore, setting $x_3=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/34e8934a56885ec189cc1819b1336f5b52b7708a092233e3c20e0960218cad10.png)\n\nFinally, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/ec378af1062dda61e89d060d565ef70bf7593a723bd3cba78dfd86f0d027dc5d.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/example-5506.json" "file_sha256" "b71888518c3c14b743bcc9a696b363817e28f54df69982927c5323a5e00b9487"} {"question" "e-7330" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/ca47231d1f55afd5641f3924cae2252af74467ba29adbbdb51832cbd4cf06663.png) \nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=2,$ $\\lambda_2=1,$ and $\\lambda_3=0.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D=P^{-1}AP.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/example-7330.json" "file_sha256" "37d6e1d661150a12bf81c27b646606354943ca402f4359bdf63b3fa57d1b1de4"} {"question" "e-7330" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=2,\\quad$ $\\lambda_2=1, \\quad$ $\\lambda_3=0.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we get the following.\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives us the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/b88fd696293e4151a71ca12d25cb56821ba034e07a876777fa30d5d6f6b9c4c3.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives us the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/38941ad5615365437a1aee93f085b33d6a240c5a61ce8e0f0a10acb465de0f18.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_3=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives us the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/a3ea884b0f6ba11582eb72e6cffcc871a144e55c446aaa304e1c7f9face13c7b.png)\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/cc87c4f07116bf301d115ffeb00e81f9cd25d21cb40f61fdbc310f86557a61b4.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/example-7330.json" "file_sha256" "37d6e1d661150a12bf81c27b646606354943ca402f4359bdf63b3fa57d1b1de4"} {"question" "q-37275" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "da044bdbd5d6ff06c9fc50eb1245a3e7846d7db7154556afcdb985e5671b9450" "reviewer_session" "01a118cc-89a0-7c90-9221-d6599ae13bca" "rationale" "Derived judgment: the columns of P are v1=[0,-1,1], v2=[1,0,2], and v3=[1,2,1]. Direct multiplication gives Av1=-2v1, Av2=-v2, and Av3=v3, so AP=PD. Also det(P)=-3, hence P is invertible and A=PDP^-1. Original option 2 and the authentic worked solution agree. Incorrect grading concerns the submitted 'A is not diagonalizable', shown under Your Answer. Stored and captured matrices have identical entries and ordering." "bindings" {"problem" "de962b242e6b6e42ff25073187acabdec4e0fd4ac2e40a4c7ff0e23a28474e5a" "worked_solution" "2b44013f6d46dbda994c4d29ac09ef8ab3bf5adc54cddf095181c1948b2bc2c9" "original_field" "8467c4ffea88d83219ca3524007b2089dd613ff63b828e0251912cde5270c852" "reviewed_field" "832f6709fc32c0563d3a374bb3597d1c644372a1e31672c6d03f66b13b5cc7b7" "raw_widget" "963f8c9b666c10f2a834fa90c518815240278a694ca11b3680fe2724e5afe8d6"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-37275-before.png" "sha256" "43b2c73747f80ae390593cc3bb62e77572347899c11bdf59371997356827ff52"} {"path" "history-q-37275.png" "sha256" "8e6f82fb614d35f1649fe1d79783575342ae0c6cec3e69f18a786450f9008814"} {"path" "q-37275-after.json" "sha256" "9042cf5194f2bc911f5261a8f33c004641cddd6694d0671f734a82cde293be0f"}]} {"question" "q-37275" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$A$ is not diagonalizable"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "da044bdbd5d6ff06c9fc50eb1245a3e7846d7db7154556afcdb985e5671b9450" "rationale" "Derived judgment: the columns of P are v1=[0,-1,1], v2=[1,0,2], and v3=[1,2,1]. Direct multiplication gives Av1=-2v1, Av2=-v2, and Av3=v3, so AP=PD. Also det(P)=-3, hence P is invertible and A=PDP^-1. Original option 2 and the authentic worked solution agree. Incorrect grading concerns the submitted 'A is not diagonalizable', shown under Your Answer. Stored and captured matrices have identical entries and ordering."}] "usage" {"e-5505" [] "e-5504" [] "q-37273" [] "e-5506" [] "q-37275" [] "e-7330" [] "q-37250" []} "basis" 2417 "retractions" [[17592186124959 :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, D = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}, P = \\begin{bmatrix}∗ & 1 & 1 \\\\ ∗ & 0 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nGiven that $D = P^{-1}AP$, find the first column of the matrix $P$."] [17592186124965 :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, D = \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}, P = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & a & 1 \\\\ 1 & b & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nGiven that $A = PDP^{-1}$, find the value of $\\frac{a}{b}$."] [17592186124966 :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -12 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues $λ_{1} =-2$, $λ_{2} =-1$, and $λ_{3} = 1$.\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $A = PDP^{-1}$."] [17592186124966 :question/answer-fields 17592186122818] [17592186124259 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which $\\lambda_1=-1$, $\\lambda_2=1$, $\\lambda_3=0$ are eigenvalues, and\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & b & 0 \\\\ a & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186124259 :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}-1}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2={\\color{red}1}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_3=0\n$$\n\nwe construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P$, we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns:\n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}-1}$,\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}1}$, and\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_3=0$.\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we get $a=0$, $b=-1$, and $c=-1$."] [17592186124260 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nDetermine whether $A=PDP^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D$, where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D$."] [17592186124260 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $\\mathbb R$.\n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A$. To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A - λI) &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 - λ & 6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 - λ & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 4 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(4 - λ)\\begin{vmatrix}2 - λ & 6 \\\\ 2 & 1 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(4 - λ)([λ^{2} - 3λ + 2] - 12) &= 0 \\\\\n(4 - λ)(λ^{2} - 3λ - 10) &= 0 \\\\\n(4 - λ)(λ - 5)(λ + 2) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=4\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lambda_3=-2\n$$\n\nSince all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable.\n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186124261 :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -9 & -7 & 9 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 14\\end{bmatrix}$\nGiven that $D=P^{-1}AP$, find the first column of the matrix $P$."] [17592186124261 :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=-2, \\quad\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=-1, \\quad\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_3=2\n$$\n\nSo, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=-2\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=-2\n$$\n\nComputing $A-(-2)I$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - λ_{1}I &= \\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -9 & -7 & 9 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 14\\end{bmatrix} - (-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -9 & -5 & 9 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 16\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -9 & -5 & 9 & 0 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 16 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-\\frac{3}{2})R_{1} \\\\ R_{3}:=R_{3} + (-\\frac{8}{3})R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{3} & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{vmatrix}R_{3}:=R_{3} + (-\\frac{2}{3})R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain $x_2=0$. Substituting this into the first equation, we get $x_1=x_3$.\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $x_3=1$, we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186124303 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=2$, $\\lambda_2=1$, and $\\lambda_3=0$.\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D=P^{-1}AP$."] [17592186124303 :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.** Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix:\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=2,\\quad\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=1, \\quad\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_3=0\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A$. Applying our usual method, we get the following.\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=2$, we get\n$A - 2I|= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives us the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- For $\\lambda_2=1$, we get\n$A - I|= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives us the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- For $\\lambda_3=0$, we get\n$A - 0I|= A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives us the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186122777 17592186122778 17592186122779 17592186122780 17592186122781 17592186122813 17592186122814 17592186122815 17592186122816 17592186122817 17592186122819 17592186122820 17592186122821 17592186122822 17592186122823] "immutable_fields" [[17592186122776 :answer-field/correct] [17592186122776 :answer-field/id] [17592186122776 :answer-field/key] [17592186122776 :answer-field/type] [17592186122812 :answer-field/correct] [17592186122812 :answer-field/id] [17592186122812 :answer-field/key] [17592186122812 :answer-field/type] [17592186122818 :answer-field/correct] [17592186122818 :answer-field/id] [17592186122818 :answer-field/key] [17592186122818 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
